  "query_preview": "..A gradual downturn in the coverage and relative \nintensity of shower and thunderstorm activity is anticipated over \nthe next couple of days starting today. However, any storms that do \nform will continue to present a concern for heavy rainfall rates \n(0.75 to 1 inch per hour), frequent lightning, and possibly even \ngusty outflows to around 35 mph. Showers and storms have already \nformed with adequate instability (500-1000 J/kg MLCAPE) in \nplace as CIN erodes from east to west across the area. The lower\nportions of the atmosphere remain saturated despite drier air \npunching in aloft with precipitable water estimated via \nsatellite between around 1 to 1.4 inches which is supportive of \nheavier downpours from the strongest storms.\n\nTomorrow and Saturday storm chances decrease with a slight chance \n(10-20%) mainly focused from the Tucson Metro east and south and \nover the higher terrain. Drier air aloft could act to suppress \ncoverage and intensity of storms across the area, but surface \nmoisture will remain in play. Although I anticipate there to be \nnotably less coverage than today, the residual low level moisture \ndoes present a little bit of uncertainty.\n\nAs the upper high shifts, heat comes back into play by early next \nweek with HeatRisk increasing to moderate to locally major across \nsoutheast Arizona. Definitely something we continue to watch as the \npattern evolves over the next several days. An Extreme Heat \nWatch in currently in effect from late Monday morning through \nWednesday evening. High temperatures right now are forecast to \ntop out around 4 to 8 degrees above normal (or roughly 104 to \n109 degree range within the Extreme Heat Watch). Temperatures \nmay not feel shockingly different than any other hot summer day \nin southeast Arizona, but this is the point in the season where \nwe expect normal daytime high temperatures to dip just below 100\ndegrees in the Tucson Metro.\n\nEarly next week chances for showers and storms look to return as an \neasterly wave moves over northern Mexico. This would bring some \nadded moisture and dynamics back into play supporting a potential \nuptick in storm activity mainly east and south of Tucson. The added \nmoisture could actually end up moderating those aforementioned \nhigh temperatures, but storm activity for now looks to remain \nfocused mostly south and east of the Tucson Metro until the \nmiddle of next week.",
